# Minimal, conservative SwiftFormat config for MedTrackerCore.
#
# Goal: catch genuine whitespace/import-hygiene drift without rewriting the
# TS-ported source. A handful of SwiftFormat's default-on rules would touch
# large swaths of intentionally-styled or parity-sensitive code, so they're
# turned off here rather than fought file-by-file:
#
# - wrapIfStatementBodies: would explode the single-line `if cond { ... }`
#   style used consistently across the ported modules (Analytics, Time,
#   Inventory, Reminders, Schedule) into multi-line form.
# - numberFormatting: would add underscore grouping to numeric literals
#   (timestamps, thresholds) across sources and tests — cosmetic churn with
#   no readability win for this codebase's numeric literals.
# - redundantRawValues: would strip explicit raw values from enum cases
#   (e.g. `ScheduleKind`) that are deliberately spelled out together for
#   TS wire-format parity, even where one happens to match Swift's implicit
#   default.
# - noForceUnwrapInTests / hoistTry: would rewrite test assertions from
#   force-unwrap (`!`) to `try #require(...)`, which changes test function
#   signatures (adds `throws`) — a structural rewrite, not a format fix.
# - docComments: would convert plain `//` narrative comments (test-authorship
#   notes, TS-source cross-references) into `///` doc comments, changing
#   their semantics rather than just their formatting.
# - conditionalAssignment: would collapse `let x: T; if cond { x = a } else
#   { x = b }` blocks into Swift 5.9 `let x = if cond { a } else { b }`
#   expression syntax — a real control-flow restructuring (Time.swift,
#   Csv.swift), not a whitespace/formatting fix.
# - blankLinesAroundMark: this codebase's `// MARK: - Title (...)` headers
#   routinely wrap their description onto a second `//` line (a deliberate,
#   pervasive style — see Schedule.swift, ScheduleTests.swift, TimeTests.swift,
#   InventoryTests.swift). The rule assumes MARK comments are single-line and
#   inserts a blank line immediately after the "MARK:" line, splitting the
#   wrapped sentence in two rather than adding a blank line after the whole
#   comment block.
--swiftversion 5.9
--disable wrapIfStatementBodies,numberFormatting,redundantRawValues,noForceUnwrapInTests,hoistTry,docComments,conditionalAssignment,blankLinesAroundMark
